###Pure
// legal-netting -- the netting set: the population one master agreement covers, and the unit
// exposure is actually measured on.
//
// The one sentence the whole project is a consequence of: netting is not a property of a
// trade and not a property of a counterparty. It is a property of a SET, and the set is
// defined by a contract. Two trades net against each other because they are governed by the
// same single agreement between the same two legal persons -- not because they face the same
// name, not because they are the same product. So a NetSet is the closure of a master
// agreement, and everything downstream of exposure measurement has to be grouped on it.
//
// The second sentence: the set only nets FOR CAPITAL if a clean netting opinion covers it.
// The opinion lives upstream in legal-core, keyed on the exact triple (jurisdiction, master
// form, counterparty type). What this project adds is the ASSESSMENT -- the firm's own
// determination, on a date, that a particular opinion reaches a particular set, branch by
// branch. Absent that determination the set still exists and is still measured; it is just
// measured GROSS, and NetCapitalTreatment is where that shows.
//
// Layer 2. Depends on legal-core (the agreement and the opinion) and exposure-agg (the
// exposure being netted), and on nothing else.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the set itself

// The population one master agreement covers: the unit exposure is measured on.
//
// Keyed by its own id rather than by the agreement id, even though the two are one-to-one in
// the ordinary case, because they are NOT one-to-one in the cases that matter -- an umbrella
// master with per-fund netting sets underneath it, and a master whose set has been split
// because one product line turned out not to be covered by the opinion.
Class legal_netting::NetSet
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   // The master agreement this set is the closure of. The association NetSetAgreement is the
   // navigable form; the column is kept because the set is built from the agreement id before
   // the graph is walked.
   agreementId: String[1];
   // The counterparty the set faces. One set, one counterparty -- if two names appear the set
   // is wrong, not merely large.
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // The insolvency regime the counterparty is subject to: BANK, INSURER, FUND, CORPORATE,
   // SOVEREIGN, PENSION. Half of the opinion lookup key, and the half a trade system never
   // knows, which is why it is carried here rather than derived at query time.
   counterpartyType: String[1];
   // The jurisdiction whose insolvency law governs the counterparty. NOT the governing law of
   // the agreement: an English-law ISDA with a German bank is opined on under German
   // insolvency law, and conflating the two is how a set gets netted that should not be.
   counterpartyJurisdiction: String[1];
   // CLOSE_OUT, PAYMENT_ONLY or NONE. Payment netting is settlement plumbing and earns no
   // capital relief; close-out netting is the one that reduces exposure at default.
   nettingBasis: String[1];
   // The currency the close-out is struck in, taken from the agreement's termination currency.
   settlementCurrency: String[1];
   // The construction run that built this set. Optional because a set hand-created by a
   // credit officer for a workout is still a set.
   runId: String[0..1];
   establishedDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Set when the set is closed out or the agreement terminates, never cleared -- historical
   // exposure on a closed set stays attributable to it.
   closedDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   isCrossProductSet: Boolean[1];

   // Live until closed. The absence of the date is the test.
   isOpen() { $this.closedDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Only ever a claim about the CONTRACT. Whether it nets for capital is a different
   // question, answered by NetCapitalTreatment after an opinion has been assessed.
   nettableByContract() { $this.nettingBasis == 'CLOSE_OUT' } : Boolean[1];
}

// One exposure line inside a set, on one date: the membership fact.
//
// Keyed (set, exposure, date) and not (set, exposure), because membership is a daily
// statement. A trade novated away on Tuesday was in the set on Monday, and Monday's netted
// exposure has to stay reproducible.
//
// This is the class that reaches exposure-agg. It restates no exposure numbers of its own
// beyond the two it is measured on -- the aggregate below sums them, and a third copy
// somewhere else would be a third answer.
Class legal_netting::NetSetMember
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   // The reach into exposure-agg. The association NetMemberExposure is the navigable form.
   exposureId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // TRADE, COLLATERAL, UNPAID_AMOUNT. Collateral is a member of the set because it is netted
   // against the trades inside it, not deducted after the set is totalled.
   memberKind: String[1];
   productClass: String[1];
   // Exposure before any offset -- what is owed if the set does not net.
   grossExposure: Float[1];
   // Signed. Negative is a payable, and the sign is the entire point: a set of positives is
   // not a netting set, it is a list.
   signedMarkToMarket: Float[1];
   // Whether THIS line is inside the netted population. False for a line the opinion does not
   // reach, which stays in the set for reporting and out of the net for capital.
   isNettable: Boolean[1];
   // The rule that decided isNettable, so the decision is attributable.
   eligibilityRuleCode: String[0..1];
   includedOn: StrictDate[1];

   // A payable to the counterparty is what makes netting worth anything.
   isPayable() { $this.signedMarkToMarket < 0.0 } : Boolean[1];
}

// The rule that decides whether a line is inside the netted population.
//
// Kept as data rather than as code because the answer changes with the opinion, not with the
// release: when counsel confirms that repo under a GMRA nets against the cash leg, the change
// is a row here and a re-run, and the pre-change numbers stay explicable.
Class legal_netting::NetEligibilityRule
{
   ruleCode: String[1];
   ruleName: String[1];
   productClass: String[1];
   // The master form the rule is scoped to; a rule true for the 2002 ISDA is not thereby true
   // for a GMRA.
   typeCode: String[0..1];
   jurisdictionCode: String[0..1];
   includesInNetting: Boolean[1];
   // The paragraph of the opinion the rule is taken from. A rule with no basis is a guess.
   opinionBasis: String[0..1];
   effectiveFrom: StrictDate[1];
   effectiveTo: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// A product, currency or jurisdiction carved OUT of an otherwise netted set.
//
// Distinct from a rule that never included it: an exclusion is a set-specific carve-out, and
// it is the shape a qualified opinion actually takes -- "netting is enforceable except in
// respect of transactions with the counterparty's Singapore branch".
Class legal_netting::NetExclusion
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   exclusionCode: String[1];
   exclusionScope: String[1];
   reason: String[1];
   // Points back at the qualification in the legal-core opinion that forced the carve-out.
   sourceQualification: String[0..1];
   raisedOn: StrictDate[1];
   resolvedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   isActive() { $this.resolvedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the opinion behind it

// The firm's determination, on a date, that a legal-core netting opinion reaches this set.
//
// The opinion upstream is a legal document about a triple. THIS is the act of applying it:
// somebody with a name checked that this set's counterparty really is of the opined type in
// the opined jurisdiction under the opined form, and signed. Capital relief rests on this row
// existing and being current, which is why it carries a review date and an expiry rather than
// a boolean.
//
// Keyed (set, assessedOn) so the history is append-only. Last year's assessment is what last
// year's capital number was computed against.
Class legal_netting::NetOpinionAssessment
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   assessedOn: StrictDate[1];
   // The three columns that identify the upstream legal_core::NettingOpinion. Carried in full
   // because the opinion's key is three columns and a single surrogate would hide which of
   // the three stopped matching when the counterparty was reclassified.
   opinionJurisdiction: String[1];
   opinionTypeCode: String[1];
   opinionCounterpartyType: String[1];
   // The conclusion. False means the set is measured gross, whatever the agreement says.
   supportsNetting: Boolean[1];
   // Clean means unqualified. A qualified opinion is not a netting opinion for capital, and
   // any qualification that survives assessment turns into a NetExclusion above.
   isClean: Boolean[1];
   assessedBy: String[1];
   // Opinions are refreshed annually. An assessment past its review date is stale, and stale
   // is treated as absent.
   nextReviewDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   assessmentNote: String[0..1];

   // What the capital rules actually require: an opinion that is both supportive and clean.
   givesCapitalRelief() { $this.supportsNetting && $this.isClean } : Boolean[1];
}

// Whether the opinion reaches one booking branch of the set.
//
// Keyed (set, branch), and it exists because a multibranch master is the ordinary case and
// the opinion is jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction. A set booked through London, Singapore and New
// York needs three opinions, and the weakest one governs: a branch the opinion does not reach
// is exposure measured gross inside a set that otherwise nets.
Class legal_netting::NetBranchCoverage
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   branchCode: String[1];
   branchJurisdiction: String[1];
   isCoveredByOpinion: Boolean[1];
   // The provider whose opinion covers this branch. Different branches are routinely covered
   // by different firms.
   opinionProvider: String[0..1];
   coverageFrom: StrictDate[1];
   coverageTo: StrictDate[0..1];
   qualificationNote: String[0..1];
}

// The netting recognition actually applied to a set on a date, and the number it produced.
//
// Keyed (set, date). Separate from the assessment because the assessment is a legal fact and
// this is an accounting one: they can disagree, and when they do the disagreement is the
// finding. `recognisedAsNet` false with a clean assessment on file means something else --
// a stale review, an unresolved exclusion, an uncovered branch -- blocked it.
Class legal_netting::NetCapitalTreatment
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Denormalised from the set, and carried on purpose: the counterparty rollup below groups
   // on it, and a view cannot group on a column it would have to join to reach.
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   // The decision. False means the gross number below is the exposure that goes to capital.
   recognisedAsNet: Boolean[1];
   // Both numbers are held, not one and a derivation, because the benefit is the DIFFERENCE
   // and a supervisor asks for it directly.
   grossExposureAmount: Float[1];
   netExposureAmount: Float[1];
   collateralValueApplied: Float[1];
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   // ORIGINAL_EXPOSURE, SA_CCR, IMM -- the method the netted number was computed under.
   exposureMethod: String[1];
   // Why netting was refused, when it was. Null when recognisedAsNet is true.
   denialReason: String[0..1];

   // The capital benefit of the set, in reporting currency. Both operands are Float, so the
   // subtraction stays Float.
   nettingBenefit() { $this.grossExposureAmount - $this.netExposureAmount } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ close-out

// The single net sum owed when the set is closed out: the number the whole construction
// exists to produce.
//
// One per (set, determination date). The mechanics -- Market Quotation versus Close-out
// Amount, First versus Second Method, the grace period -- are upstream in
// legal_core::CloseOutTerms and are not restated here; the association NetCloseOutBasis is
// how this row reaches them. What is here is the calculation and its result.
Class legal_netting::NetCloseOutAmount
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   determinedOn: StrictDate[1];
   // Carried so this row can join to the agreement's close-out terms directly, without a hop
   // through NetSet -- the determination is made by a defaults desk working from the
   // agreement, not from the set.
   agreementId: String[1];
   // The date the termination was designated, which is not the date it was calculated. The
   // gap between them is where the dispute usually is.
   earlyTerminationDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Whether the counterparty or the firm defaulted. Decides who determines and who pays.
   defaultingParty: String[1];
   // BANKRUPTCY, FAILURE_TO_PAY, CROSS_DEFAULT, ADDITIONAL_TERMINATION_EVENT.
   triggerEvent: String[1];
   // Signed, from the determining party's perspective. Positive is owed TO the firm.
   closeOutAmount: Float[1];
   // Amounts that fell due before termination and were never paid. Part of the single sum,
   // and a separate line because they rank differently in some insolvency regimes.
   unpaidAmounts: Float[1];
   // Collateral held, applied against the sum. Whether it may be applied at all depends on
   // the annex's title-transfer election upstream.
   collateralApplied: Float[1];
   terminationCurrency: String[1];
   isDisputed: Boolean[1];
   // Set when the amount has been settled. Until then it is a claim, not a payment.
   settledOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   // The single sum: close-out plus unpaids less collateral. All Float, so the arithmetic
   // stays Float.
   netTerminationAmount()
   {
      $this.closeOutAmount + $this.unpaidAmounts - $this.collateralApplied
   } : Float[1];
}

// One line of the close-out calculation, so the number above can be defended.
//
// Keyed (set, determination date, component). A close-out amount that cannot be broken back
// into the quotations and valuations that produced it is not a close-out amount, it is an
// assertion -- and it is the assertion the counterparty's counsel attacks first.
Class legal_netting::NetCloseOutComponent
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   determinedOn: StrictDate[1];
   componentCode: String[1];
   // TERMINATION_VALUE, UNPAID_AMOUNT, FUNDING_COST, COLLATERAL_CREDIT.
   componentKind: String[1];
   // The exposure line this component values, when it values one. Null for set-level lines
   // like the funding cost.
   exposureId: String[0..1];
   componentAmount: Float[1];
   componentCurrency: String[1];
   // DEALER_QUOTATION, INTERNAL_MODEL, MID_MARKET. Which of the three was used is exactly
   // what the 2002 form's commercial-reasonableness standard is judged on.
   valuationSource: String[1];
   quotationCount: Integer[0..1];
   determinedBy: String[0..1];
}

// A right to set off the close-out sum against amounts owed under a DIFFERENT agreement.
//
// Deliberately not a property of the set: set-off reaches ACROSS sets, which is what
// distinguishes it from netting, and modelling it inside one set would lose that. Keyed by
// its own id because a right is between two agreements and belongs to neither.
Class legal_netting::NetSetOffRight
{
   setOffId: String[1];
   // The set whose close-out sum is being set off.
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   // The other agreement the amount is set off against. Not a NetSet id: the other side is
   // often a loan or a custody balance that has no netting set at all.
   againstAgreementId: String[1];
   setOffScope: String[1];
   // Contractual set-off elected in the Schedule, versus a set-off arising under the general
   // law. The second is far weaker and jurisdiction-dependent.
   isContractual: Boolean[1];
   // Whether counsel confirmed the right survives the counterparty's insolvency. An
   // unconfirmed set-off right earns nothing.
   isEnforceableOnInsolvency: Boolean[1];
   governingLawCode: String[1];
   establishedOn: StrictDate[1];
   exercisedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the run

// One execution of the set-construction process: which sets were built, from what, when.
//
// Netting sets are derived, and a derived population needs a batch that owns it. Without this
// there is no answer to "why was that trade not in the set yesterday", which is the question
// asked every time the netted number moves.
Class legal_netting::NetSetRun
{
   runId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   runType: String[1];
   startedAt: DateTime[1];
   completedAt: DateTime[0..1];
   setCount: Integer[1];
   memberCount: Integer[1];
   // Sets whose opinion assessment was missing or stale on this run. Not an error -- those
   // sets went to capital gross, which is correct and expensive.
   unopinionedSetCount: Integer[1];
   runStatus: String[1];
   triggeredBy: String[0..1];

   // Members per set. `/` widens Integer to Float on its own, so this is Float[1].
   averageSetSize() { $this.memberCount / $this.setCount } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ THE AGGREGATION

// Exposure rolled up to the set: one row per set per date, over the member lines.
//
// This is the shape of the whole project's answer. Members are per-trade and there are
// millions; the netting set is what a limit, a capital number and a margin call are all
// written against, and it is six figures fewer rows. The class is rooted on a store VIEW
// whose ~groupBy is (set, date), so an instance IS a group and never a member -- and the
// GROUP BY is folded into the generated SQL rather than materialised.
//
// The two totals are kept side by side on purpose: `totalGross` sums every member,
// `netExposure` sums only the ones the opinion reaches. Where they differ, the difference is
// either an exclusion or an uncovered branch, and it is visible here in one row instead of
// being reconstructed from the member lines.
Class legal_netting::NetSetRollup
{
   nettingSetId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   memberCount: Integer[1];
   // Sum of grossExposure over every member of the set.
   totalGross: Float[1];
   // Sum of signedMarkToMarket -- signed, so this is the netted figure and can be negative.
   netExposure: Float[1];
   // The largest single member. A set whose exposure is one trade does not really net, and
   // this is the column that shows it without opening the members.
   largestMemberGross: Float[1];
}

// The same exposure rolled up one level higher, to the counterparty across all its sets.
//
// A second aggregation and not a refinement of the first: sets do NOT net against each other,
// so this row is a SUM of set-level nets and never a net across them. Keeping it separate
// from NetSetRollup is what stops the two being confused -- the number here is what the
// credit officer sees, and it is legitimately larger than netting the same trades in one set
// would give.
Class legal_netting::NetCounterpartyRollup
{
   counterpartyId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   setCount: Integer[1];
   totalGross: Float[1];
   // Sum over sets of each set's net. NOT the net across sets, which would be wrong.
   sumOfSetNets: Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ associations
//
// The internal spine first: everything hangs off NetSet, which is the point of a netting set.

Association legal_netting::NetSetMembers
{
   memberSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   members: legal_netting::NetSetMember[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetSetAssessments
{
   assessedSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   opinionAssessments: legal_netting::NetOpinionAssessment[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetSetBranches
{
   coveredSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   branchCoverage: legal_netting::NetBranchCoverage[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetSetExclusions
{
   excludedSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   exclusions: legal_netting::NetExclusion[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetSetTreatment
{
   treatedSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   capitalTreatments: legal_netting::NetCapitalTreatment[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetSetCloseOut
{
   closedOutSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   closeOutAmounts: legal_netting::NetCloseOutAmount[*];
}

// Two-column join on the way down: a component belongs to one determination, not to every
// determination the set has ever had.
Association legal_netting::NetCloseOutBreakdown
{
   closeOutAmount: legal_netting::NetCloseOutAmount[1];
   components: legal_netting::NetCloseOutComponent[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetSetOffRights
{
   setOffSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   setOffRights: legal_netting::NetSetOffRight[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetMemberRule
{
   eligibilityRule: legal_netting::NetEligibilityRule[0..1];
   ruledMembers: legal_netting::NetSetMember[*];
}

Association legal_netting::NetRunSets
{
   buildingRun: legal_netting::NetSetRun[1];
   builtSets: legal_netting::NetSet[*];
}

// The aggregate hangs off the set it aggregates, so a query can go set -> rollup without
// touching a member row.
Association legal_netting::NetSetRollups
{
   rolledUpSet: legal_netting::NetSet[1];
   rollups: legal_netting::NetSetRollup[*];
}

// ---- ACROSS THE BOUNDARY into legal-core --------------------------------------------------
//
// The three reaches that make a netting set a legal object rather than a grouping key: the
// agreement that DEFINES the set, the opinion that lets it net, and the close-out terms that
// say how the sum is struck.

// The defining association. A master agreement has netting sets; the ordinary case is one,
// and an umbrella master has several.
Association legal_netting::NetSetAgreement
{
   nettingAgreement: legal_core::MasterAgreement[1];
   nettingSets: legal_netting::NetSet[*];
}

// The assessment points at the opinion it applied. Three-column join upstream, because the
// opinion's key is three columns and any two of them match the wrong document.
Association legal_netting::NetAssessedOpinion
{
   appliedOpinion: legal_core::NettingOpinion[0..1];
   setAssessments: legal_netting::NetOpinionAssessment[*];
}

// The close-out row reaches the Schedule elections that govern how it was calculated.
Association legal_netting::NetCloseOutBasis
{
   closeOutBasis: legal_core::CloseOutTerms[0..1];
   determinedCloseOuts: legal_netting::NetCloseOutAmount[*];
}

// ---- ACROSS THE BOUNDARY into exposure-agg -------------------------------------------------
//
// The other half of the theme. legal-netting says what the set IS; exposure-agg says what it
// is WORTH. Neither restates the other: there is no gross number in exposure-agg's shape here
// and no opinion in this project's arithmetic, and the two meet on `nettingSetId` alone.

// The measured exposure, per run, for the set. To-many because a set is measured every day
// and more than once on a restatement.
//
// This is the association the whole project points at: a netting set is the unit exposure is
// measured on, and here is the measurement.
Association legal_netting::NetSetNetExposure
{
   governingLegalSet: legal_netting::NetSet[0..1];
   nettedExposures: exposure_agg::ExaNetExposure[*];
}

// Member to the gross line it turned into. Two-column join, on the set AND the exposure,
// because an exposure id is only unique within a set here -- the same underlying credit
// exposure can sit in a second set after a novation, and matching on it alone merges two
// different measurements.
Association legal_netting::NetMemberExposureLine
{
   exposureLine: exposure_agg::ExaExposureLine[0..1];
   nettingSetMembers: legal_netting::NetSetMember[*];
}
